-- title:
--   Inertia-equivariant one-point mover on X₀(Nq)
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime, $A$ a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, $N$ a positive integer, $k$ an algebraically closed field of characteristic $q$, and $\mathrm{red}\colon A\to k$ a ring homomorphism. Write $\bar F_{Nq}=$ `modularFunctionFieldBar (N * q)` for the base change to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the full modular function field of level $Nq$, $F^k_N=$ `modularFunctionFieldC k N` for the subfield of $k$-Laurent series generated by the $q$-expansions $j(q)$ and $j(q^N)$, and $F^{\kappa_A}_N=$ `modularFunctionFieldFullC (ResidueField A) N` for the full level-$N$ field over the residue field of $A$. For a place $v$, $\mathrm{ord}_v$ is minus the logarithm of its adic valuation, $L(D)=$ `riemannRochSpace D` is $\{f : v(f)\le \exp(D\,v)\ \forall v\}$, and $g=$ `genusFF k` $F^k_N$ is the genus of $F^k_N$ over $k$.
--
--   The data fixed are: `data`, modular polynomial data at level $q$ (a monic $\Phi\in\mathbb Z[X][Y]$ of degree $\psi(q)$ with $\Phi(j,j_q)=0$); `hKr`, the Kronecker congruence $\Phi\equiv (X^q-Y)(X-Y^q) \pmod q$; `hα` and `hβ`, the integrality of the two degeneracy maps `heckeAlphaBar` and `heckeBetaBar` from the level-$N$ to the level-$Nq$ base-changed fields; and `P`, a place specialisation of the indicated type, that is a map `P.sp` from places of the level-$N$ field over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ to places of $F^k_N$, a homomorphism on degree-zero divisor class groups, and the laws relating values and poles of $j$ and $j_N$ upstairs to those downstairs through $\mathrm{red}$. For a place $V$ of $\bar F_{Nq}$, `P.reduceFst V` and `P.reduceSnd V` are `P.sp` applied to the restrictions of $V$ along `heckeAlphaBar` and `heckeBetaBar` respectively. Write $\varphi=$ `frobOnPlacesGeomLevel k N data hKr` for the map on places of $F^k_N$ obtained by restricting a place to the image of $F^k_N$ under the $q$-expansion map (a subfield over which $F^k_N$ is integral, by the Kronecker congruence) and transporting it back along the isomorphism of $F^k_N$ onto that image. A place $V$ of $\bar F_{Nq}$ is strict-first, `P.IsStrictFst V`, when $\varphi(\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V)=\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,V$ and $\varphi^2(\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V)\ne \mathrm{reduceFst}\,V$, and strict-second, `P.IsStrictSnd V`, when $\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V=\varphi(\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,V)$ and $\varphi^2(\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,V)\ne\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,V$; a divisor is good, `P.IsGoodDiv`, when every place of its support is strict-first or strict-second, and `P.fstDiv D`, `P.sndDiv D` are the restrictions of $D$ to its strict-first, respectively strict-second, part. A place $v$ of $F^k_N$ is affine, `IsAffineGeomPlace k N v`, when both generators $j(q)$ and $j(q^N)$ lie in its valuation subring; `ssPlaces q N k` is the set of places satisfying the predicate `IsSupersingularPlace q N k`, called supersingular below. Standing assumptions: $\bar F_{Nq}$ has principal divisors and is a curve over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, and $F^k_N$ is a curve over $k$ (finite residue extensions and a free rank-one module of Kähler differentials).
--
--   The remaining data are a prolongation tuple $R$ over $P$ — comprising a homomorphism `R.redBar` from the residue field of $A$ to $k$ lifting $\mathrm{red}$, a ring homomorphism `R.ι`$\colon F^{\kappa_A}_N\to F^k_N$ acting by `R.redBar` on Laurent coefficients, two regular prolongations `R.R₁`, `R.R₂` of $A$ to $\bar F_{Nq}$ with residue field $F^{\kappa_A}_N$, and the compatibility laws identifying their rings of integers through the localised modular ring and the Atkin–Lehner involution and expressing the second residue by the first — together with the following hypotheses, whose contents are summarised here.
--
--   `hR`: $R$ is a model, i.e. the conjunction of `R.DivisorLawFst`, `R.DivisorLawSnd`, `R.CuspLawInfty`, `R.CuspLawZero`. `hO`: the fixed-place order law, namely for $f$ lying in the integers of both prolongations with both residues non-zero and for any divisor $D$ with $D\,V=\mathrm{ord}_V f$ for all $V$, every $\varphi^2$-fixed affine place $v$ of $F^k_N$ satisfies $\big((\mathrm{reduceFst})_*D\big)(v)=\mathrm{ord}_v(\mathrm{residue}_1 f)+\mathrm{ord}_{\varphi(v)}(\mathrm{residue}_2 f)$. `W` with `hW`: a finite set whose members are exactly the supersingular places. `hRL`: the regularity law at $W$ (two clauses: non-negativity of the two residue orders at $\varphi^2$-fixed affine places all of whose strict-first preimages are non-poles of $f$, and existence of a common value of the two residues at a node pair under the corresponding hypothesis). `hNV`: the node-value law at $W$ (for bi-integral $f$ with non-zero residues, a node pair not met by the divisor of $f$ carries a common non-zero value of the two residues). `hqN`: $q\nmid N$.
--
--   The cusp and side hypotheses: `hcusp`, every place $V$ of $\bar F_{Nq}$ whose first reduction is not affine is cuspidal, i.e. $\mathrm{ord}_V(\,j$-function $-\,a)\le 0$ for every $a\in A$; `hsplit`, every cuspidal $V$ is on the $\infty$-side or on the $0$-side (the predicates `ProlongationTuple.IsInftySide`, `ProlongationTuple.IsZeroSide`, asking cuspidality, respectively the predicate `IsCuspidal'`, together with $\tau\in A$ with $\mathrm{red}\,\tau=1$ at which the parameter `tInfty N q`, respectively `tZero N q`, takes the value $\tau$ at $V$); `hInftyNA`, `hInftyNA'`, for $V$ on the $\infty$-side neither reduction is affine; `hZeroNA`, `hZeroNA'`, likewise for $V$ on the $0$-side. `hφaff`: $\varphi$ preserves affineness of places. `hNss`: both coordinates of every node pair in `nodePairsOfPlaces (arithFrobC q k N) W` (the finite set attached to $W$ by the coefficient-wise arithmetic Frobenius semilinear automorphism of $F^k_N$) are supersingular.
--
--   `hzone`, the zone hypothesis: for every finite set $T$ of non-supersingular places of $F^k_N$ there is a finite set $Z$ with $T\subseteq Z$, $\varphi(v)\in Z$ for $v\in T$, $v\in Z$ whenever $\varphi(v)\in T$, every non-affine place in $Z$, every non-supersingular $\varphi^2$-fixed place in $Z$, and every member of $Z$ non-supersingular.
--
--   `hAUX`, the auxiliary-points hypothesis: for every finite set $B$ of places of $F^k_N$ and all $m_1,m_2\in\mathbb N$ there are families $Q_1\colon\mathrm{Fin}\,m_1\to$ places of $\bar F_{Nq}$ and $Q_2\colon\mathrm{Fin}\,m_2\to$ places of $\bar F_{Nq}$ with all $Q_1 i$ strict-first, all $Q_2 j$ strict-second, the maps $i\mapsto \mathrm{reduceFst}(Q_1 i)$ and $j\mapsto\mathrm{reduceSnd}(Q_2 j)$ injective with images avoiding $B$, and every $Q_1 i$ and $Q_2 j$ fixed by `arithmeticGalois` $\sigma$ for every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup `A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ`.
--
--   `hINTERP`, the interpolation hypothesis: for all finite sets $U,Z_v,Z_a$ of places of $F^k_N$, a place $t_0$, $\beta\in k$, a function $\mathrm{val}$ from places to $k$ and a finite set $\mathrm{bad}\subseteq k$, if $U,Z_v,Z_a$ are pairwise disjoint, $t_0$ lies in none of them and $|Z_v|+2g+2\le|U|$, then there is $h\in L\big(\sum_{u\in U}u\big)$ with $\mathrm{ord}_{t_0}(h-\beta)=1$, with $h$ taking the value $\mathrm{val}(z)$ at each $z\in Z_v$, and with $h$ taking at each $z\in Z_a$ some value outside $\mathrm{bad}$.
--
--   `hREALN`, the equivariant realisation hypothesis: for every set $S$ of automorphisms of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ over $\mathbb Q$ contained in `A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ`, every effective good divisor $D$ on $\bar F_{Nq}$ whose support is fixed pointwise by `arithmeticGalois` $\sigma$ for all $\sigma\in S$ and which satisfies $2g-1+|W|\le \deg\big((\mathrm{reduceFst})_*\mathrm{fstDiv}\,D\big)$ and $2g-1\le\deg\big((\mathrm{reduceSnd})_*\mathrm{sndDiv}\,D\big)$, and every pair $g_1,g_2\in F^{\kappa_A}_N$ with $\iota g_1\in L\big((\mathrm{reduceFst})_*\mathrm{fstDiv}\,D\big)$, $\iota g_2\in L\big((\mathrm{reduceSnd})_*\mathrm{sndDiv}\,D\big)$ and with, for each node pair $s$ of $W$, a common value $c\in k$ of $\iota g_1$ at $s_1$ and of $\iota g_2$ at $s_2$: there exists $G\in\bar F_{Nq}$, integral for both prolongations, with $G\in L(D)$, with `R.R₁`-residue $g_1$ and `R.R₂`-residue $g_2$, and fixed by `arithmeticGalois` $\sigma$ for all $\sigma\in S$.
--
--   Finally, a map $\mathrm{toFull}\colon F^k_N\to F^{\kappa_A}_N$ with `R.ι` $\circ\,\mathrm{toFull}=\mathrm{id}$, a finite set $T$ of places of $F^k_N$ all of which are non-supersingular, and a place $V_0$ of $\bar F_{Nq}$ at least one of whose two reductions lies in $T$.
--
--   Under these hypotheses there exist a finite set $Z$ of places of $F^k_N$, an element $f\in\bar F_{Nq}$ lying in the rings of integers of both `R.R₁` and `R.R₂`, and a divisor $D$ on $\bar F_{Nq}$ such that:
--
--   (1) $T\subseteq Z$, every non-affine place lies in $Z$, every non-supersingular place fixed by $\varphi^2$ lies in $Z$, and every member of $Z$ is non-supersingular;
--   (2) $f\ne 0$, and both reductions `R.residue₁` of $f$ and `R.residue₂` of $f$, taken in $F^k_N$, are non-zero;
--   (3) $D\,V=\mathrm{ord}_V f$ for every place $V$ of $\bar F_{Nq}$, and $D\,V_0=1$;
--   (4) every $V$ with $D\,V<0$ is either strict-first with $\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V\notin Z$ or strict-second with $\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,V\notin Z$;
--   (5) $\mathrm{ord}_z(\mathrm{residue}_1 f)=0$ for every $z$ in $Z$ or in $W$ with $z\ne\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V_0$, and $\mathrm{ord}_z(\mathrm{residue}_2 f)=0$ for every $z$ in $Z$ or in $W$ with $z\ne\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,V_0$;
--   (6) either $\mathrm{ord}_{\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V_0}(\mathrm{residue}_1 f)=1$ and $\mathrm{ord}_{\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,V_0}(\mathrm{residue}_2 f)=0$, or $\mathrm{ord}_{\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V_0}(\mathrm{residue}_1 f)=0$ and $\mathrm{ord}_{\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,V_0}(\mathrm{residue}_2 f)=1$;
--   (7) no place $V\ne V_0$ with $D\,V\ge 1$ has both reductions in $Z$;
--   (8) every $V$ in the support of $D$ other than $V_0$ has neither reduction in $T$;
--   (9) for every $\sigma$ in `A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ`, if `arithmeticGalois` $\sigma$ fixes $V_0$ then it fixes $f$.
--
--   This is the core existence statement of the one-point moving step on the level-$Nq$ curve: it produces a function whose divisor has a simple zero at a prescribed place $V_0$, whose poles avoid a Frobenius-stable zone $Z$ of non-supersingular places, whose two reductions on the components of the special fibre have the prescribed order table, and which is fixed by those inertia elements of $A$ that fix $V_0$. It is used by [`ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.ProlongationTuple.exists_ord_eq_one_forall_isStrict_reduceFst_reduceSnd_notMem_forall_inertia_smul_eq_of_isModel`](thm.html#ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.ProlongationTuple.exists_ord_eq_one_forall_isStrict_reduceFst_reduceSnd_notMem_forall_inertia_smul_eq_of_isModel).
